  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2}) & =(-2)^{3}+2(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
